00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:59.240
understanding English. And so I've had

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:01.200
it where the thumbnail is almost right,

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:03.400
and then you talk to the thumbnail tool

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.840
and it just completely messes it up. It

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:06.680
just makes it actually like 10 times

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:08.160
worse. So a pro tip here is if you don't

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:09.600
get it right on the first try, just

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:11.240
start over and then just change your

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:14.200
prompt a little bit. All right, number

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:16.600
five is ViddIQ. And this one is really

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:17.960
good, especially for keyword research,

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:19.320
right? And most people are doing this

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:21.240
wrong. Now, ViddIQ has been around

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:23.400
forever, but they've leaned really hard

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:25.720
into AI lately, and a few of the newer

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:27.880
features are genuinely useful. And the

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:30.560
one I use the most is called related

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:32.360
keywords. So it basically uses AI to map

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:34.520
out which keywords are semantically

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:37.280
connected across YouTube. And basically,

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:39.280
ViddIQ has processed billions of YouTube

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:41.640
search queries and learned which terms

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:43.440
the algorithm treats as linked together.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:45.880
Another way of saying this is which

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:47.320
terms are semantically related to each

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:49.120
other. And that's a fancy way of saying

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:51.200
if a person is interested in this

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:52.800
keyword, they're also very likely going

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:54.839
to be interested in this other keyword.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:56.520
So, just as an example, if you look up

00:10:54.839 --> 00:10:58.040
remote jobs, Shane Hummus is going to

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:59.800
show up near the top. If you look up

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:01.480
Shane Hummus, remote jobs is going to

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:03.920
show up near the top. But you might also

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:05.839
notice that side hustles is semantically

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:07.240
related to remote jobs. So this is

00:11:05.839 --> 00:11:09.360
actually incredibly useful to know that

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:10.760
if somebody's interested in remote jobs,

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:13.040
there's a very good chance they're also

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:14.440
interested in side hustles as well. And

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:16.800
when you look up one keyword, it

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:18.520
surfaces the cluster of related ones

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:20.400
that the algorithm already groups it

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:22.400
with. And that sounds boring until you

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:23.839
actually see what it does. So, why does

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:24.920
this matter? Well, if I want to target

00:11:23.839 --> 00:11:26.520
someone that's interested in remote

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:28.400
jobs, ViddIQ tells me that they're

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:31.080
probably also interested in side

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:33.200
hustles, freelancing, financial freedom,

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:35.520
passive income, etc., etc. And that

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:37.560
tells me what videos I should make next.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:39.280
And it also tells me what not to make,

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:41.680
which is honestly just as valuable. And

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:43.280
VidIQ also has a separate AI keyword

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:45.040
generator. And this is where you give it

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:46.880
a niche, and it spits out keywords with

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:48.600
search volume and competition scores

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:50.080
attached. And it's one of the best uses

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:51.520
I found to generate keyword research

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:52.920
with VidIQ. Then you just plug the

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:54.400
keywords into one of 10 to find the

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:55.640
actual videos, and these would be the

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:57.000
videos that are getting clicks on those

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:58.280
terms. And if you stack these two,

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:01.000
you've got an AI doing your topic

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:03.040
research and a real data tool validating

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:04.960
it. So, I found VidIQ suggestions to

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:06.880
actually be pretty accurate, which I do

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:08.440
not say about most AI tools. All right,

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:09.800
so number six is going to be FireCut.

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:11.400
And this is for the Adobe Premiere

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:13.760
people out there. So, if you're using

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:16.280
Adobe Premiere Pro to basically do your

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:18.520
editing, FireCut is a plugin that auto

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:20.880
cuts your raw footage. Now, it's not

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:22.600
quite as smooth as Descript, but if

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:24.160
you're already living in Premiere Pro,

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:26.240
it can auto cut your footage and

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:28.160
probably save you about 30 minutes to an

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:29.960
hour each time. And it's going to ensure

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:31.880
that you don't have to switch workflows.

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:33.640
So, FireCut saves you the hour that

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:35.360
you'd otherwise spend trimming filler

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:36.720
manually. So, you push a button and it's

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.280
done. And then you get to move on.

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:39.839
Pretty cool. And again, there's a lot of

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:41.600
tools out there that claim to do this,

00:12:39.839 --> 00:12:43.600
but almost all of them make the editing

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:45.400
super choppy. FireCut was one of the

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:47.280
only ones we tested out of probably

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:48.480
about a dozen of them that actually had

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:49.960
pretty good editing. All right, now the

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:51.640
last section is the one that I think

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:53.720
most of you have been waiting for, and

